Consider an interacting system of $N$ identical fermions evolving under a (possibly time-dependent) Hamiltonian. For each $p\ge 1$, let $\rho_p(t)$ denote the time-dependent $p$-body reduced density matrix obtained by tracing the full $N$-body density matrix over $N-p$ particles. The matrices $\rho_p(t)$ satisfy the BBGKY hierarchy, in which the equation of motion for $\rho_p(t)$ couples to $\rho_{p+1}(t)$.

A closure (truncation) at the two-body level is a prescription that expresses $\rho_3(t)$ as a functional $\mathcal{F}[\rho_1(t),\rho_2(t)]$ so that the coupled equations for $\rho_1(t)$ and $\rho_2(t)$ become a closed system. Call such a closure admissible if, for every initial datum $(\rho_1(0),\rho_2(0))$ arising from a fermionic $N$-body pure or mixed state and for all $t\ge 0$, the propagated matrices $\rho_1(t)$ and $\rho_2(t)$ satisfy:

(i) Positive semidefiniteness: $\rho_1(t)\succeq 0$ and $\rho_2(t)\succeq 0$;

(ii) Fermionic occupation constraint: every eigenvalue (natural orbital occupation number) of $\rho_1(t)$ lies in $[0,1]$;

(iii) Physical local density: the diagonal $n(x,t)=\rho_1(x,x;t)$ is non-negative everywhere and remains finite (no divergences) for all $t\ge 0$;

(iv) Conservation of trace (particle number) and Hermiticity of $\rho_1(t),\rho_2(t)$.

Problem. Construct a non-trivial closure functional $\mathcal{F}[\rho_1,\rho_2]$ for the truncated two-body BBGKY hierarchy that is admissible in the above sense (in particular, that goes beyond mean-field/Hartree–Fock and includes genuine two-body correlations), and prove that the resulting closed evolution propagates the constraints (i)–(iv) exactly, without recourse to ad hoc post-hoc purification or filtering.

